WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.285
Lesson 4.0.

00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:05.260
The Mastery Starter for Module 4: Yield.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.064
By the end, you'll know the five

00:00:07.064 --> 00:00:09.196
words this module runs on, and exactly

00:00:09.196 --> 00:00:11.164
how to tell when you've mastered it.

00:00:12.464 --> 00:00:13.754
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.754 --> 00:00:17.118
First, the sixty-second version: what every single yield

00:00:17.118 --> 00:00:19.779
you'll ever see is actually payment for.

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:22.093
Second, the five words you'll need, each

00:00:22.093 --> 00:00:23.804
one drawn out on its own.

00:00:23.804 --> 00:00:26.219
Third, what to have ready before you start.

00:00:26.219 --> 00:00:29.443
Fourth, your first safe step: splitting one

00:00:29.443 --> 00:00:33.114
real, advertised A.P.Y. into its actual parts.

00:00:33.114 --> 00:00:35.629
And finally, the mastery ladder, so you know

00:00:35.629 --> 00:00:38.199
exactly what finishing this module looks like.

00:00:39.499 --> 00:00:42.064
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:00:42.064 --> 00:00:45.011
Every yield you'll ever come across is payment

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:48.084
for something specific: staking pays you to help

00:00:48.084 --> 00:00:50.968
secure a network, lending pays you for taking

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:53.727
on borrower demand, and farms often pay you

00:00:53.727 --> 00:00:56.849
in newly printed tokens, to attract deposits.

00:00:56.849 --> 00:00:59.435
This module teaches you to see exactly what

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:01.609
any given yield is really paying for.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.572
Here's the mechanism underneath every source

00:01:05.572 --> 00:01:07.705
of yield in this entire module.

00:01:07.705 --> 00:01:11.839
Real, organic yield comes from genuine economic activity:

00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:15.332
trading fees paid by real traders, interest paid

00:01:15.332 --> 00:01:18.468
by real borrowers, or a network paying real

00:01:18.468 --> 00:01:20.835
value for real security work.

00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:24.268
Subsidised yield, by contrast, comes from a protocol

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:28.219
printing brand-new tokens and handing them out, specifically

00:01:28.219 --> 00:01:31.045
to attract deposits it wants right now.

00:01:31.045 --> 00:01:33.174
Both can show up as the exact

00:01:33.174 --> 00:01:36.155
same-looking A.P.Y. number on a dashboard.

00:01:37.455 --> 00:01:39.997
This distinction is the single most useful

00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:42.421
skill this entire module builds, so it's

00:01:42.421 --> 00:01:44.850
worth sitting with before moving on.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.191
Two protocols can advertise the exact same double-digit A.P.Y.,

00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:53.125
and mean completely different things by it: one genuinely

00:01:53.125 --> 00:01:56.516
earned from real activity, durable for as long as

00:01:56.516 --> 00:02:01.264
that activity continues; the other manufactured from token emissions,

00:02:01.264 --> 00:02:04.927
and only as durable as the protocol's willingness, or

00:02:04.927 --> 00:02:06.690
ability, to keep printing.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.455
The same number, two very different sources.

00:02:11.755 --> 00:02:14.624
Here's a purely illustrative example, just to

00:02:14.624 --> 00:02:16.932
make the shape of this concrete, not

00:02:16.932 --> 00:02:19.790
a claim about any specific real protocol.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:23.350
Imagine a headline A.P.Y. of thirty percent.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.257
Split honestly, that might actually be eight percent genuinely

00:02:27.257 --> 00:02:31.164
earned from real trading activity, and twenty-two percent paid

00:02:31.164 --> 00:02:34.317
out purely in emissions, newly printed tokens.

00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:35.892
Same headline number.

00:02:35.892 --> 00:02:38.047
Very different durability underneath it.

00:02:39.346 --> 00:02:42.468
Worth naming the actual habit this hypothetical builds,

00:02:42.468 --> 00:02:45.221
since you'll use it constantly from here on.

00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:49.188
Whenever a headline number looks unusually attractive compared to

00:02:49.188 --> 00:02:53.096
similar products elsewhere, that gap is almost always emissions,

00:02:53.096 --> 00:02:56.221
not some newly discovered source of free money.

00:02:56.221 --> 00:02:58.895
The honest question is never simply how big

00:02:58.895 --> 00:03:01.023
the number is; it's how much of it

00:03:01.023 --> 00:03:04.001
survives once the printing eventually slows down.

00:03:05.301 --> 00:03:08.237
And here's the shape those two components tend

00:03:08.237 --> 00:03:11.360
to take over time, again purely illustrative, not

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.971
a prediction about any real protocol.

00:03:13.971 --> 00:03:16.979
The organic portion tends to stay roughly flat,

00:03:16.979 --> 00:03:20.036
since it's tied to steady, ongoing activity.

00:03:20.036 --> 00:03:23.159
The emissions portion tends to decay, month after

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:25.658
month, as more of the reward token gets

00:03:25.658 --> 00:03:28.144
printed, sold, and its price falls.

00:03:28.144 --> 00:03:30.679
Six months in, the headline number has

00:03:30.679 --> 00:03:33.474
usually fallen a long way, almost entirely

00:03:33.474 --> 00:03:35.684
because the emissions portion did.

00:03:36.984 --> 00:03:38.759
Now, the five words you'll need.

00:03:40.059 --> 00:03:43.544
Word one: A.P.Y., annual percentage yield.

00:03:43.544 --> 00:03:46.419
Here's what actually goes into that single number.

00:03:46.419 --> 00:03:48.679
It starts with your base rate of return.

00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:51.249
It adds the effect of compounding, meaning

00:03:51.249 --> 00:03:54.299
reinvesting what you've already earned so far.

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:56.545
And the result is a yearly rate that

00:03:56.545 --> 00:03:59.641
already assumes you keep reinvesting the whole way

00:03:59.641 --> 00:04:02.555
through, not just a simple, one-time percentage.

00:04:03.855 --> 00:04:05.740
Word two: emissions.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:09.215
New reward tokens, printed directly by a protocol,

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:12.757
and handed out to whoever deposits, specifically to

00:04:12.757 --> 00:04:15.510
attract deposits it wants right now.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.199
Emissions aren't necessarily a red flag on

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:20.701
their own; plenty of protocols use them

00:04:20.701 --> 00:04:23.690
deliberately, to bootstrap early liquidity.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:26.282
But they're a cost the protocol is choosing

00:04:26.282 --> 00:04:28.343
to pay, in its own token, and that

00:04:28.343 --> 00:04:30.817
token's price very often falls as more of

00:04:30.817 --> 00:04:32.290
it gets printed and sold.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.375
Word three: staking.

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:38.020
Here's the actual sequence behind this one.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.054
You lock up tokens, specifically to help secure a

00:04:41.054 --> 00:04:44.570
network, most often a blockchain's own validator set.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.178
The network relies on that locked value to

00:04:47.178 --> 00:04:50.995
make attacking or corrupting it economically unattractive.

00:04:50.995 --> 00:04:53.031
And in exchange for taking on that

00:04:53.031 --> 00:04:55.066
role, and its risks, you're paid a

00:04:55.066 --> 00:04:57.160
yield, funded by the network itself.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.990
Word four: L.S.T., a liquid staking token.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.501
When you stake through many modern platforms, instead of

00:05:05.501 --> 00:05:09.506
your original tokens simply disappearing into a locked, illiquid

00:05:09.506 --> 00:05:12.894
position, you receive an L.S.T. back: a separate token

00:05:12.894 --> 00:05:16.713
representing your staked position, which you can still trade,

00:05:16.713 --> 00:05:19.608
move, or use elsewhere in DeFi, all while your

00:05:19.608 --> 00:05:22.935
underlying stake keeps earning its own separate yield.

00:05:24.235 --> 00:05:27.860
Word five: R.W.A., a real-world asset.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.401
A token representing something that exists entirely outside crypto,

00:05:32.401 --> 00:05:36.876
most commonly a short-term government treasury bill, but sometimes

00:05:36.876 --> 00:05:41.984
real estate, private credit, or other traditional financial instruments.

00:05:41.984 --> 00:05:45.985
R.W.A. yield is attractive specifically because it's tied

00:05:45.985 --> 00:05:50.399
to real-world interest rates, largely independent of whatever's

00:05:50.399 --> 00:05:53.779
happening in crypto markets that particular week.

00:05:55.079 --> 00:05:56.169
Quick check.

00:05:56.169 --> 00:05:58.712
What does an L.S.T. actually let you do

00:05:58.712 --> 00:06:01.254
that plain staking, on its own, doesn't?

00:06:05.674 --> 00:06:08.607
The answer: it lets you keep trading, moving, or

00:06:08.607 --> 00:06:11.960
using your staked position elsewhere in DeFi, while the

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.654
underlying stake keeps earning its own yield.

00:06:15.954 --> 00:06:18.601
Here's L.S.T. and R.W.A. side by side,

00:06:18.601 --> 00:06:21.384
since they're easy to blur together, but

00:06:21.384 --> 00:06:24.196
come from genuinely different worlds.

00:06:24.196 --> 00:06:28.004
An L.S.T. represents a position staked inside crypto

00:06:28.004 --> 00:06:32.041
itself, earning yield the network pays for security.

00:06:32.041 --> 00:06:36.531
An R.W.A. represents something entirely outside crypto, most

00:06:36.531 --> 00:06:40.806
often earning yield tied to real-world interest rates.

00:06:40.806 --> 00:06:43.222
Both hand you a token you can trade or

00:06:43.222 --> 00:06:46.443
use elsewhere; what backs that token, and what risk

00:06:46.443 --> 00:06:49.541
it's actually exposed to, is completely different.

00:06:50.842 --> 00:06:51.932
Quick check.

00:06:51.932 --> 00:06:55.497
What's the actual difference between organic yield and emissions?

00:06:59.917 --> 00:07:03.366
The answer: organic yield comes from real economic

00:07:03.366 --> 00:07:06.980
activity, like trading fees or borrower interest.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:09.640
Emissions are new tokens a protocol prints

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.610
and hands out, specifically to attract deposits.

00:07:13.909 --> 00:07:14.954
Before you start.

00:07:16.254 --> 00:07:18.273
Here's what this module assumes you're

00:07:18.273 --> 00:07:20.799
already comfortable with, before diving in.

00:07:20.799 --> 00:07:24.639
Everything from Module zero: wallets, networks, and gas.

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:27.447
Module one's due-diligence habits, for checking

00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:29.619
any protocol before touching it.

00:07:29.619 --> 00:07:33.784
Module two's read on A.M.M.s, liquidity, and MEV.

00:07:33.784 --> 00:07:36.997
And Module three's lending and leverage concepts,

00:07:36.997 --> 00:07:39.761
since several yield sources in this module

00:07:39.761 --> 00:07:42.524
build directly on borrowing and collateral.

00:07:43.824 --> 00:07:45.099
Your first safe step.

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:49.150
Here's exactly what to do, before this module

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.081
goes any deeper into specific yield sources.

00:07:52.081 --> 00:07:56.131
Pick any protocol currently advertising a yield you've noticed.

00:07:56.131 --> 00:07:59.147
Open its own dashboard, and find the breakdown it

00:07:59.147 --> 00:08:02.946
actually publishes, most protocols do show this somewhere.

00:08:02.946 --> 00:08:05.132
Separate what it calls base yield from

00:08:05.132 --> 00:08:07.562
what it calls incentives or emissions.

00:08:07.562 --> 00:08:09.537
And write down, in your own words,

00:08:09.537 --> 00:08:11.737
what each part is actually payment for.

00:08:13.037 --> 00:08:16.095
Worth doing this exercise on a real number, not

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:19.598
a hypothetical one, since the entire skill this module

00:08:19.598 --> 00:08:23.465
builds is reading real dashboards under real conditions.

00:08:23.465 --> 00:08:26.393
A yield that turns out to be almost entirely

00:08:26.393 --> 00:08:30.427
emissions isn't automatically a scam; it's simply telling you

00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:33.811
honestly what you'd actually be exposed to, and for

00:08:33.811 --> 00:08:36.479
how long that exposure is likely to last.

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:39.931
And here's what to do specifically if a

00:08:39.931 --> 00:08:42.674
protocol doesn't publish a clean breakdown at all,

00:08:42.674 --> 00:08:44.319
since not all of them do.

00:08:44.319 --> 00:08:47.323
Check the reward token's own emission schedule,

00:08:47.323 --> 00:08:50.077
most protocols document how many new tokens

00:08:50.077 --> 00:08:52.129
get released, and how often.

00:08:52.129 --> 00:08:56.137
Compare deposit growth against that emission schedule; deposits

00:08:56.137 --> 00:08:58.767
rising in step with new token releases is

00:08:58.767 --> 00:09:02.699
a strong signal you're looking at incentive-driven growth.

00:09:02.699 --> 00:09:05.596
And if you genuinely can't tell, treat the entire

00:09:05.596 --> 00:09:08.204
yield as if it were emissions, until you can.

00:09:09.504 --> 00:09:10.599
The mastery ladder.

00:09:11.899 --> 00:09:14.026
Here's the first two rungs on this

00:09:14.026 --> 00:09:16.336
module's ladder, side by side, so you

00:09:16.336 --> 00:09:18.824
can see exactly what separates them.

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:21.096
At the beginner level, you can tell base

00:09:21.096 --> 00:09:23.700
yield apart from emissions on any given yield,

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.494
the core skill this whole module starts with.

00:09:26.494 --> 00:09:29.797
At the practitioner level, you go further: comparing

00:09:29.797 --> 00:09:32.789
a managed vault against doing the same strategy

00:09:32.789 --> 00:09:36.466
yourself, and correctly pricing points programs or airdrop

00:09:36.466 --> 00:09:38.834
farming as the bets they actually are.

00:09:40.134 --> 00:09:42.889
Here's what the practitioner level actually looks

00:09:42.889 --> 00:09:44.872
like in practice, one level up from

00:09:44.872 --> 00:09:47.651
just telling base yield apart from emissions.

00:09:47.651 --> 00:09:51.049
First, you compare a managed vault's advertised yield

00:09:51.049 --> 00:09:55.139
against running that same underlying strategy yourself directly,

00:09:55.139 --> 00:09:57.516
factoring in the vault's own fee.

00:09:57.516 --> 00:10:00.563
Then, when a protocol offers points, or hints

00:10:00.563 --> 00:10:03.809
at a future airdrop, you price that specifically

00:10:03.809 --> 00:10:05.929
as a bet with real odds, not as

00:10:05.929 --> 00:10:08.446
guaranteed extra yield stacked on top.

00:10:09.746 --> 00:10:12.063
And here's the top rung, master level,

00:10:12.063 --> 00:10:15.036
the one this module ultimately builds toward.

00:10:15.036 --> 00:10:18.539
At that level, you can construct yield deliberately from

00:10:18.539 --> 00:10:23.393
durable, well-understood sources: lending markets, staking, savings rates, and

00:10:23.393 --> 00:10:28.248
tokenised treasuries, choosing each source specifically because you understand

00:10:28.248 --> 00:10:31.197
exactly what risk it's paying you for, not just

00:10:31.197 --> 00:10:33.471
because the number looked attractive.

00:10:34.771 --> 00:10:37.457
Here's how you'll actually know you've mastered

00:10:37.457 --> 00:10:39.771
this entire module, in one sentence.

00:10:39.771 --> 00:10:42.349
You can look at any yield, anywhere, and name

00:10:42.349 --> 00:10:45.095
both its source and the specific risk being paid

00:10:45.095 --> 00:10:48.402
for, without needing to check a dashboard breakdown first,

00:10:48.402 --> 00:10:50.756
because you already recognise the pattern.

00:10:52.056 --> 00:10:53.146
Quick check.

00:10:53.146 --> 00:10:54.829
What does the master level of

00:10:54.829 --> 00:10:56.961
this module's ladder actually require?

00:11:01.381 --> 00:11:05.772
The answer: building yield deliberately from durable sources, like

00:11:05.772 --> 00:11:10.361
lending, staking, savings rates, and tokenised treasuries, chosen for

00:11:10.361 --> 00:11:14.031
their understood risk, not just their advertised number.

00:11:15.331 --> 00:11:16.576
Let's recap.

00:11:16.576 --> 00:11:18.842
Every yield is payment for something

00:11:18.842 --> 00:11:22.081
specific, organic activity or printed emissions.

00:11:22.081 --> 00:11:26.159
The five words: A.P.Y., emissions, staking, L.S.T.,

00:11:26.159 --> 00:11:28.903
and R.W.A. Your first safe step is

00:11:28.903 --> 00:11:32.589
splitting one real, advertised A.P.Y. into its

00:11:32.589 --> 00:11:36.574
actual parts, using the protocol's own numbers.

00:11:36.574 --> 00:11:39.292
And you'll know you've mastered this module when

00:11:39.292 --> 00:11:41.344
you can name the source and the risk

00:11:41.344 --> 00:11:43.784
behind any yield, in one sentence, on sight.

00:11:45.084 --> 00:11:48.827
Pick one real, advertised APY today, and split it

00:11:48.827 --> 00:11:53.394
into its actual parts using the protocol's own dashboard.

00:11:53.394 --> 00:11:55.588
Next up, Lesson 4.1: yield

00:11:55.588 --> 00:11:58.269
farming, base yield vs emissions.
